  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nLittle to no change since the last forecast issuance. Rain continues \nto spread in at the current hour and a transition from rain to snow \nis expected to occur this afternoon into the evening hours across \nthe Cumberland Plateau, southern east TN mountains, and southwest\nNC. For these areas, decided to issue an SPS to convey the threat\nof poor visibilities due to the potential of moderate to heavy \nsnowfall rates. Although minor accumulations of generally an inch \nor less are possible, and a few slick spots, the reduced \nvisibilities and Thanksgiving travel aspect remain the main \nconcern. The areas that could see the most accumulation will be \nthe higher elevations of Monroe County, TN and higher elevations \nof Cherokee and Clay NC. These areas are included in the above \nmentioned SPS. 1 to 2 inches of snow are possible with isolated \namounts up to 3 inches, again, for the highest elevations in those\ncounties. \n\nThe rest of the east TN and southwest VA mountains, higher \nelevations, may see minor accumulations to around 0.5 to 1 inch. \nAs mentioned, some valley locations from the southern TN valley up\nthrough the northern TN valley will see some flakes flying around\nat some point as well. However, the ground will likely be too \nwarm for any accumulation. These forecast snowfall totals continue\nto agree with the latest HI-RES model runs. Because of this, \nstill no winter weather advisory due to the low probability of \neven minor impacts.\n\nThe threat for gusty winds hasn't changed either. Strong and \ngusty winds are expected to occur from this afternoon into \ntomorrow across the entire area. The strongest gusts are expected \neast of I-75 across portions of the central and northern valley, \nthe east TN mountains and southwest VA. A Wind Advisory remains in\nplace for these areas and wind gusts from 30 up to 40 mph are \nexpected. Areas not in the wind advisory are still expected to see\ngusts from 20 to 30+ mph at times.\n\nMost areas are precip free by Friday morning. However, lingering \nprecip is expected to occur across northeast TN and southwest VA\nthrough Friday. Saturday will be dry with seasonable temperatures.\n\nAnother system will likely bring rain Sunday into Sunday night. \nWind fields look likely to be significant again with this system \nso strong gusty winds will be possible especially across the \nhigher elevations. Ensemble data suggests convective energy is \nlikely to be minimal, but there may be enough instability for a \nfew rumbles of thunder Sunday. \n\nAfter we clear out on Monday, the remainder of the forecast through \nWednesday looks dry with near seasonal temperatures.",
